    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Overview and detail\"\ndescription: \"Bind one panel's view to a brush on another with bind_scale(). Drag a range on the overview strip and the detail panel pans and zooms to match.\"\ncategories: [Interactive]\nimage: figs/overview-detail.png\n---\n\n\n\n`bind_scale()` ties one panel's axis to an interval selection declared on\nanother. Brush a range on an overview plot and the detail plot pans and zooms to\nthat range. The view moves without a re-render or a scale retrain, so a long\nseries stays legible: keep the whole shape in a thin strip, read the part you\ncare about up close.\n\nThis is the third thing a selection can drive, alongside the spotlight and\ncross-filter of the [declarative interactivity](declarative-interactivity.qmd)\nentry. Here the gesture changes what a panel shows rather than how it is styled.\n\n## A daily series, four hundred points long\n\nThe overview draws the full random walk. `select_interval(on = \"x\")` gives it an\nx-only brush, and `add_selection()` attaches that brush to the overview's marks.\nThe detail draws the same series and calls `bind_scale()` on the brush, binding\nits x-axis to the selected range. Drag a window on the lower strip and the upper\npanel zooms into it.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nrange <- select_interval(\"range\", on = \"x\")\n\ndetail <- vplot(walk) |>\n  mark_line(x = t, y = v, data_id = id) |>\n  bind_scale(range, aes = \"x\") |>\n  labs(title = \"Detail — follows the brush below\", x = NULL, y = \"value\")\n\noverview <- vplot(walk) |>\n  mark_line(x = t, y = v) |>\n  add_selection(range) |>\n  labs(title = \"Overview — brush a range\", x = NULL, y = NULL)\n\nconcat(\n  detail, overview,\n  design = \"\n    A\n    A\n    B\n  \"\n) |>\n  as_widget(height = 480)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div id=\"htmlwidget-d2d093bff456136fb01e\" style=\"width:576px;height:480px;\" class=\"vellumwidget html-widget\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/js